msgid ""
"&arts; and then kdelibs should be installed before everything else, and "
"kdeaddons last. The other packages can be installed in any arbitrary order."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: install.docbook:274
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Most package management tools will let you put all these packages in one "
"folder and install them all at once, figuring out the dependencies as they "
"go."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: install.docbook:283
#, no-c-format
msgid "Installation instructions for the different package formats"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: install.docbook:288
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Please do not forget to read the <filename>README</filename> and "
"<filename>INSTALL</filename> files if they are available."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: term
#: install.docbook:292
#, no-c-format
msgid "Installation of the Debian packages"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: install.docbook:294
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"The Debian packages install in accordance with the upcoming <acronym>FHS</"
"acronym> (File Hierarchy Standard)."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: install.docbook:297 install.docbook:309
#, no-c-format
msgid "become superuser"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: install.docbook:298
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"run <command>dpkg <option>-i <replaceable>packagename.deb</replaceable></"
"option></command> for every package you want to install."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: term
#: install.docbook:305
#, no-c-format
msgid "Installation of the <acronym>RPM</acronym> packages"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: install.docbook:308
#, no-c-format
msgid "To install binary <acronym>RPM</acronym>s"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: install.docbook:310
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"execute <command>rpm <option>-ivh <replaceable>packagename.rpm</"
"replaceable></option></command>"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: term
#: install.docbook:316
#, no-c-format
msgid "Installation of the source <filename>.tar.bz2</filename> files"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: install.docbook:318
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Since there are always changes and updates to the way &kde; is compiled, "
"please refer to <ulink url=\"http://developer.kde.org/build/index.html"
"\">Download and Install from Source</ulink> for the most up to date "
"installation instructions for the source packages."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: install.docbook:321
#, no-c-format
msgid "The general approach should work in most cases though."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: install.docbook:323
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"The source <filename>.tar.bz2</filename> package installs into <filename "
"class=\"directory\">/usr/local/kde</filename> by default. You can override "
"this setting by using the <option>--prefix</option> option of the "
"<filename>configure</filename> script."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: install.docbook:328
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"unpack the packages with <command>tar <option>jxvf <replaceable>packagename."
"tar.bz2</replaceable></option></command>"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: install.docbook:330
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"change folder to the package folder: <command>cd <replaceable>packagename</"
"replaceable></command>"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: install.docbook:332
#, no-c-format
msgid "configure the package: <command>./configure</command>"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: install.docbook:333
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Some packages (notably kdebase) have special configuration options that "
"might be applicable to your installation. Type <command>./configure "
"<option>--help</option></command> to see the available options."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: install.docbook:337
#, no-c-format
msgid "build the package: <command>make</command>"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: install.docbook:338
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"install the package: <command>su <option>-c \"make install\"</option></"
"command> (if you aren't already root). If you already are, just type "
"<command>make <option>install</option></command>."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: install.docbook:351
#, no-c-format
msgid "Post-installation procedures"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: install.docbook:354
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"First of all, please make sure that you have added &kde;'s binary "
"installation folder (&eg; <filename class=\"directory\">/usr/local/kde/bin</"
"filename>) to your <envar>PATH</envar> and &kde;'s library installation "
"folder to your <envar>LD_LIBRARY_PATH</envar> (only necessary on systems "
"that do not support rpath; on &Linux; &ELF;, it should work without). This "
"environment variable may be called differently on some systems, &eg; it is "
"called <envar>SHLIB_PATH</envar> on &IRIX;. Then set the environment "
"variable <envar>KDEDIR</envar> to the base of your &kde; tree, &eg; "
"<filename class=\"directory\">/usr/local/kde</filename>."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: install.docbook:366
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Please bear in mind that it is unwise to set <envar>LD_LIBRARY_PATH</envar> "
"blindly. In the vast majority of cases it is unnecessary and can do more "
"harm than good. There is a web page written by <ulink url=\"mailto:barr@cis."
"ohio-state.edu\">Dave Barr</ulink> explaining the evils of "
"<envar>LD_LIBRARY_PATH</envar> and it can be found at <ulink url=\"http://"
"www.visi.com/~barr/ldpath.html\">http://www.visi.com/~barr/ldpath.html</"
"ulink>."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: install.docbook:375
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Even though you can use most of the &kde; applications simply by calling "
"them, you can only benefit fully from &kde;'s advanced features if you use "
"the &kde; window manager and its helper programs."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: install.docbook:379
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"In order to make it easy for you, we have provided a simple script called "
"<filename>startkde</filename> which gets installed in <filename class="
"\"directory\">$<envar>KDEDIR</envar>/bin</filename> and is therefore in your "
"path."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: install.docbook:383
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Edit the file <filename>.xinitrc</filename> in your home folder (make a "
"backup copy first!), remove everything that looks like calling a window "
"manager, and insert <command>startkde</command> instead. Restart the &X-"
"Server;. If you use &kdm;/<application>xdm</application>, you will have to "
"edit the file <filename>.xsession</filename> instead of <filename>.xinitrc</"
"filename>. And if there is no <filename>.xinitrc</filename> or <filename>."
"xsession</filename> in your home folder, simply create a new one with just "
"one line containing <command>startkde</command>."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: install.docbook:391
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Some systems (notably &RedHat; &Linux;) use <filename>.Xclients</filename> "
"instead."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: install.docbook:394
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"This should present you with a new shining &kde; desktop. You can now start "
"to explore the wonderful world of &kde;. In case you want to read some "
"documentation first, there is a highly recommended <ulink url=\"http://www."
"kde.org/documentation/quickstart/index.html\">Quick Start guide</ulink> "
"available. Furthermore, every application has an online help that is "
"available via the <guimenu>help</guimenu> menu."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: install.docbook:407
#, no-c-format
msgid "Should I remove old version xyz before installing a new one?"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: install.docbook:411
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"In principle, this is not necessary. <acronym>RPM</acronym> and Debian "
"packages should take care of all dependencies."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: install.docbook:413
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"If you compile the source code yourself, you should take more care. "
"Instructions for running two different versions of &kde; on the same system "
"are given at <ulink url=\"http://developer.kde.org/build/kde2-and-kde3.html"
"\">http://developer.kde.org/build/kde2-and-kde3.html</ulink>. However, "
"please note that running two different versions of &kde; from source can "
"lead to problems if you are not careful."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: install.docbook:424
#, no-c-format
msgid "How do I start &kde;?"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: install.docbook:427
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"The most comfortable method to start &kde; is to use the <filename>startkde</"
"filename> script. Simply put the line <command>startkde</command> at the end "
"of your <filename>.xsession</filename> file (or your <filename>.xinitrc</"
"filename> or <filename>.Xclients</filename> file if you are not using &kdm; "
"or <application>xdm</application>). Please also remove the lines that start "
"your previous window manager. If there is no <filename>.xsession</filename>, "
"<filename>.xinitrc</filename>, or <filename>.Xclients</filename> in your "
"home folder, simply create a new one that contains just one line: "
"<command>startkde</command>."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: install.docbook:467
#, no-c-format
msgid "Is it possible to install &kde; in a user folder?"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: install.docbook:470
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Yes, you can install &kde; in any folder you want. What you have to do "
"depends on the kind of packages you want to install:"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: install.docbook:472
#, no-c-format
msgid "Source packages"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: install.docbook:473
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Configure and install the package using <command>configure <option>--"
"prefix=<replaceable>/users/myhome/kde</replaceable></option>; make; make "
"install</command> to install into <filename class=\"directory\">/users/"
"myhome/kde</filename>."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: install.docbook:477
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Add the following to your init files. Please note that if it is not "
"necessary for you to set <envar>LD_LIBRARY_PATH</envar>, it is better to "
"leave it out."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: install.docbook:481
#, no-c-format
msgid "For csh or tcsh:"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: programlisting
#: install.docbook:482
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"setenv KDEDIR /users/myhome/kde\n"
"\n"
"if ( $?LD_LIBRARY_PATH ) then\n"
"   setenv LD_LIBRARY_PATH $KDEDIR/lib:$LD_LIBRARY_PATH\n"
"else\n"
"   setenv LD_LIBRARY_PATH $KDEDIR/lib\n"
"endif\n"
"\n"
"if ( ! $?LIBRARY_PATH ) then\n"
"   setenv LIBRARY_PATH $LD_LIBRARY_PATH\n"
"endif"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: install.docbook:483
#, no-c-format
msgid "For <application>bash</application>:"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: programlisting
#: install.docbook:484
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"KDEDIR=/users/myhome/kde\n"
"PATH=$KDEDIR/bin:$PATH\n"
"LD_LIBRARY_PATH=$KDEDIR/lib:$LD_LIBRARY_PATH\n"
"LIBRARY_PATH=$LD_LIBRARY_PATH\n"
"export KDEDIR PATH LD_LIBRARY_PATH LIBRARY_PATH"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: install.docbook:486
#, no-c-format
msgid "<acronym>RPM</acronym> packages"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: install.docbook:487
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"<command>rpm</command> allows you to use the <option>--prefix</option> "
"option to select the folder you want to install to. For example, executing "
"<command>rpm <option>-i --prefix=<replaceable>/users/myhome/kde package.rpm</"
"replaceable></option></command> will install the package to <filename class="
"\"directory\">/users/myhome/kde</filename>."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: install.docbook:496
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Although &kde; will run from a user folder, there are some problems with "
"programs that require suid root, &eg; the programs in the kdeadmin package. "
"But since they are not meant to be run by users in the first place, this is "
"nothing to worry about."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: install.docbook:500
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"However, on systems using shadow passwords, the screensavers have to be suid "
"root to enable password access for unlocking the screen, so this option will "
"not work."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: install.docbook:509
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"<command>startkde</command> fails with <errorname>can not connect to X "
"server</errorname>. What is wrong?"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: install.docbook:513
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"You probably tried to start the X server with <command>startkde</command>. "
"The X server is started with <command>startx</command>. <command>startkde</"
"command> is the script that should be run from your <filename>.xinitrc</"
"filename>, <filename>.xsession</filename>, or <filename>.Xclients</filename> "
"to activate the window manager and the necessary server daemons for &kde;. "
"See also <xref linkend=\"startkde\"/>."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: install.docbook:524
#, no-c-format
msgid "&kde; on &AIX;?"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: install.docbook:527
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"<acronym>IBM</acronym> now officially support &kde; on &AIX;. You can find "
"details at <ulink url=\"http://www.ibm.com/servers/aix/products/aixos/linux/"
"index.html\">http://www.ibm.com/servers/aix/products/aixos/linux/index.html</"
"ulink>. There is also some older information at <ulink url=\"http://space."
"twc.de/~stefan/kde/aix.html\">http://space.twc.de/~stefan/kde/aix.html</"
"ulink>."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: install.docbook:544
#, no-c-format
msgid "&kde; on a laptop?"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: install.docbook:547
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"If you can get &X-Window; to run on your laptop, you should not have any "
"problem getting &kde; to run on it. In addition, you might find the "
"following links helpful:"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: ulink
#: install.docbook:552
#, no-c-format
msgid "http://www.linux-laptop.net/"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: ulink
#: install.docbook:555
#, no-c-format
msgid "http://www.sanpei.org/Laptop-X/note-list.html"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: install.docbook:563
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"I do not like the default &kde; folder after installation. How do I move it "
"without breaking anything?"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: install.docbook:567
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Assuming the default is <filename class=\"directory\">/opt/kde</filename> "
"and you want to move it to <filename class=\"directory\">/usr/local/kde</"
"filename>, here's what you have to do:"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: install.docbook:572
#, no-c-format
msgid "change to superuser if you aren't already"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: command
#: install.docbook:573
#, no-c-format
msgid "mv /opt/kde /usr/local/kde"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: command
#: install.docbook:574
#, no-c-format
msgid "ln -s /usr/local/kde /opt/kde"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: install.docbook:577
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"This will put all your &kde; files in <filename class=\"directory\">/usr/"
"local/kde</filename> but everything is still accessible from <filename class="
"\"directory\">/opt/kde</filename>."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: install.docbook:586
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"What files can I delete from my &kde; install folder? Can all the "
"<filename>*.h</filename>, <filename>*.c</filename> and <filename>*.o</"
"filename> files be safely removed?"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: install.docbook:591
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"There should not be any need to keep the <filename>.c</filename> and "
"<filename>.o</filename> files, but you might want to keep the <filename>.h</"
"filename> files, as they are used by includes if you ever want to compile "
"your own &kde; programs. But if you wish to add patches to the source "
"programs as they become available (rather than downloading everything "
"again), then they should stay."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: install.docbook:602
#, no-c-format
msgid "Will I lose my current settings when I upgrade &kde;?"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: install.docbook:606
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"No. In most cases &kde; will be able to transport your settings intact. You "
"may need to reenter passwords in some applications (such as &kmail; or "
"&knode;) but most other settings will be safe."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: install.docbook:610
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"There were mixed reports of results between some previous versions of &kde;. "
"To be safe, you may like to back up your entire &kde; configuration."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: install.docbook:614
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Settings are kept in the <filename class=\"directory\">$<envar>HOME</envar>/."
"kde</filename> or <filename class=\"directory\">$<envar>HOME</envar>/.kde2</"
"filename> subfolder in your home folder. Copy your old <filename class="
"\"directory\">.kde</filename>/<filename class=\"directory\">.kde2</filename> "
"folder to a backup location, install &kde; 3.2, and then copy back any "
"necessary mail and news settings. That said, most people can make a direct "
"upgrade, without removing the old <filename class=\"directory\">.kde</"
"filename> folder, without a hitch."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: install.docbook:624
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"You can override the use of <filename class=\"directory\">$<envar>HOME</"
"envar>/.kde</filename> by setting the $<envar>KDEHOME</envar> variable."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: install.docbook:632
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"I upgraded &kde; and it seemed to go fine, but when I start it, I get a "
"blank gray screen, and nothing happens. There are errors in the console "
"about DCOPserver. What's going on?"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: install.docbook:638
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"&kde; uses several temporary files during its operation. These are usually "
"to be found in the following locations:"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: install.docbook:642
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"<filename>~/.DCOPserver-*</filename> (there are usually two of these; one is "
"a symlink to the other)"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: filename
#: install.docbook:643
#, no-c-format
msgid "~/.kde/socket-<replaceable>hostname</replaceable>"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: install.docbook:644
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"<filename>~/.kde/tmp-<replaceable>hostname</replaceable></filename> which is "
"normally a symlink to the next file:"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: filename
#: install.docbook:645
#, no-c-format
msgid "/tmp/tmp-kde-<replaceable>USER</replaceable>"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: install.docbook:646
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"<filename>~/.kde/socket-<replaceable>hostname</replaceable></filename> which "
"is also normally a symlink to:"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: filename
#: install.docbook:647
#, no-c-format
msgid "/tmp/ksocket-<replaceable>USER</replaceable>"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: install.docbook:650
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"If the symlinks get broken, usually because a <command>cron</command> or "
"shutdown script is emptying out the <filename class=\"directory\">/tmp</"
"filename> folder, then strange things will happen. These files, and the "
"symlinks, will all be created automatically at the start of &kde; so you can "
"safely remove them <emphasis>while &kde; is not running</emphasis>."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: install.docbook:657
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"If you are only getting a gray screen when you start &kde;, or if you get an "
"error message telling you to <errorname>Check your installation</errorname>, "
"then shut down X and delete all the files listed above, then try to restart "
"X."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: install.docbook:659
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Normally (&ie; when not upgrading between &kde; versions) it's quite safe to "
"leave these files intact, and you may shave a few seconds off your &kde; "
"startup time by doing so."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: install.docbook:667
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Compiling kdebase gives me a <errorname>bin/sh: msgfmt: command not found</"
"errorname> error!"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: install.docbook:671
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"You need the &GNU; <application>msgfmt</application> which is part of the "
"&GNU; i18n package <application>gettext</application>. You should be able to "
"download it from any &GNU; mirror."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: install.docbook:680
#, no-c-format
msgid "How do I uninstall &kde; applications compiled from source?"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: install.docbook:684
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"You can uninstall your programs by typing <command>make uninstall</command> "
"in the folder where you did <command>make install</command>. If you have "
"already deleted that folder, then there is only one way, and it is not good: "
"go to <filename class=\"directory\">$<envar>KDEDIR</envar>/bin</filename> "
"and start deleting files one by one."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: install.docbook:690
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"If you expect to find yourself in this situation, you might want to consider "
"a program such as &GNU; <application>stow</application>, found at <ulink url="
"\"http://www.gnu.org/software/stow/stow.html\">http://www.gnu.org/software/"
"stow/stow.html</ulink>."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: install.docbook:698
#, no-c-format
msgid "What is up with &GIF; support?"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: install.docbook:701
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"This has to do with issues with Unisys' &LZW; patent. &GIF; support is "
"turned off from &Qt; 1.44 onwards by default. When you want to use &GIF;s "
"and have the relevant license, recompile &Qt; with &GIF; support. <command>./"
"configure <option>-gif</option></command>."
msgstr ""

